---
import ToolLayout from '@/layouts/ToolLayout.astro'
import RemoveDuplicateLines from '@/components/tools/text/RemoveDuplicateLines.tsx'
---

<ToolLayout slug="remove-duplicate-lines">
  <RemoveDuplicateLines client:load />

  <Fragment slot="explainer">
    <h2>How to remove duplicate lines</h2>
    <ol>
      <li>Paste your list with one item per line, or drop a text or CSV file onto the box.</li>
      <li>Choose the output: the deduplicated list, only the lines that repeat, or only the lines that appear exactly once.</li>
      <li>Decide whether case matters. For email addresses and URLs it usually does not; for identifiers and passwords it always does.</li>
      <li>Leave “Trim each line” on unless trailing spaces have a meaning in your data.</li>
      <li>Read the counters to confirm the result before you copy or download it.</li>
    </ol>

    <h2>Keep first or keep last?</h2>
    <p>
      Both options remove the same number of lines. They differ in which copy stays, and so in where it
      appears in the output. <strong>Keep first</strong> keeps the earliest occurrence, for a chronological
      log or a list in priority order. <strong>Keep last</strong> keeps the most recent occurrence, for data
      where later entries are updates, such as an append-only export where the newest row for a customer is
      correct.
    </p>
    <p>
      In both cases the remaining lines stay in their original order. The command-line method
      <code>sort | uniq</code> sorts everything alphabetically.
    </p>

    <h2>The three modes</h2>
    <div class="overflow-x-auto">
      <table>
        <thead>
          <tr>
            <th>Mode</th>
            <th>Input</th>
            <th>Output</th>
          </tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr>
            <td>Remove duplicates</td>
            <td>a, b, a, c, b</td>
            <td>a, b, c</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td>Show only duplicates</td>
            <td>a, b, a, c, b</td>
            <td>a, b (one entry per repeated value)</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td>Show only unique lines</td>
            <td>a, b, a, c, b</td>
            <td>c (the lines that appear exactly once)</td>
          </tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>

    <h2>Uses</h2>
    <ul>
      <li>Mailing lists: merge exports from two systems and remove the overlap before an import that charges per contact.</li>
      <li>Keyword research: combine exports from several tools and keep one row per phrase.</li>
      <li>Log analysis: “show only duplicates” with counts turns a raw log into a frequency table for a spreadsheet.</li>
      <li>Redirect maps and URL lists: find the paths that appear twice before they cause a redirect loop.</li>
      <li>Data cleanup: “show only unique lines” finds the rows that are in one export and not in the other.</li>
    </ul>

    <h2>What counts as the same line</h2>
    <p>
      Two lines are duplicates when their comparison keys match. The key is the line itself, optionally
      trimmed of leading and trailing whitespace and optionally lowercased. Nothing else is normalised:
      <code>john.doe@example.com</code> and <code>johndoe@example.com</code> are different lines, and so
      are values with different internal spacing. For looser matching, normalise the list with
      <a href="/find-and-replace">find and replace</a> first, then remove duplicates.
    </p>

    <h2>Large lists and privacy</h2>
    <p>
      The tool reads lines in chunks and lets the browser repaint between them, so a list of several hundred
      thousand lines does not freeze the tab. A customer list or a set of internal hostnames stays on your
      device.
    </p>
  </Fragment>
</ToolLayout>
